   "source": [
    "# Water Ripples Without a Sine Wave\n",
    "\n",
    "**PROG1301 · Credit Key · LT 1 & 2**\n",
    "\n",
    "A water surface on a grid. Two height buffers, one update rule, and no trigonometry anywhere in the simulation. Drop something in and the disturbance spreads, overlaps with itself, loses energy, and eventually goes flat.\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook has four parts:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. The model, and why a grid can produce wave behaviour without ever writing `sin` or `cos`.\n",
    "2. The engine, small enough to read in one sitting.\n",
    "3. Four experiments that test whether it actually behaves like water.\n",
    "4. An interactive panel for changing the simulation parameters without touching the code.\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything runs on `numpy` and `matplotlib`. No other dependency is required."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-01",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. The model\n",
    "\n",
    "A real surface displacement $u(x, y, t)$ obeys the two-dimensional wave equation\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial t^2} = c^2 \\nabla^2 u\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $c$ is the wave speed and $\\nabla^2 u = u_{xx} + u_{yy}$ measures how much a point sits below or above the average of its neighbours.\n",
    "\n",
    "The usual textbook move is to solve this equation analytically, which produces sines and cosines. We do the opposite. We keep the differential equation and replace both derivatives with finite differences, so the computer never needs the closed-form solution at all.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Space.** Put the surface on a square grid with spacing $\\Delta x$. The Laplacian at cell $(i,j)$ becomes the difference between the cell and the average of its four neighbours:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\nabla^2 u \\approx \\frac{u_{i+1,j} + u_{i-1,j} + u_{i,j+1} + u_{i,j-1} - 4u_{i,j}}{\\Delta x^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "That is five additions and one multiply. Note what is missing: nothing here knows what a wave looks like.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time.** Approximate the second time derivative with a central difference over a step $\\Delta t$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{u^{n+1} - 2u^{n} + u^{n-1}}{\\Delta t^2} = c^2 \\nabla^2 u^{n}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Rearrange for the only unknown, the next height:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "u^{n+1} = 2u^{n} - u^{n-1} + C^2 \\left( u_{i+1,j} + u_{i-1,j} + u_{i,j+1} + u_{i,j-1} - 4u_{i,j} \\right)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "with the dimensionless number\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C = \\frac{c\\,\\Delta t}{\\Delta x}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The whole simulation is that one line applied to every cell, over and over. Each new height depends on the cell's own last two heights and the current heights of its four neighbours.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stability\n",
    "\n",
    "The scheme is explicit, so it can blow up. The von Neumann analysis for this stencil gives the condition\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C \\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.707\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "in two dimensions. Physically it says the numerical front may not move more than about 0.7 of a cell per step, otherwise the update reads information faster than the grid can carry it. The engine refuses to start above that limit rather than producing silent garbage.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Damping\n",
    "\n",
    "Real water loses energy to viscosity, surface tension and the container. We add a velocity loss term, giving\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "u_{tt} + 2\\gamma u_t = c^2 \\nabla^2 u\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and discretise it with the same central differences:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "u^{n+1} = \\frac{2u^{n} - (1-\\gamma)u^{n-1} + C^2 \\nabla^2 u^{n}}{1 + \\gamma}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "A per-cell $\\gamma$ field lets us use a small uniform loss everywhere plus a stronger absorbing band at the rim, so outgoing rings leave the domain instead of bouncing off an invisible wall.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why this is not cheating\n",
    "\n",
    "The ban is on generating waves with trigonometric functions, not on the wave equation. Sines are a *solution* of the equation, not a requirement for producing it. Solving the same equation numerically gives the same physics, including dispersion, interference and the $1/\\sqrt{r}$ amplitude falloff of a circular wave, without a single trigonometric call."
   ]
  },
  {
