    "## 4. A single drop\n",
    "\n",
    "A stone hitting water does not add volume. It pushes a crater into the surface, and the displaced water has to go somewhere, so the rim rises. The initial condition in the engine is built that way: a narrow Gaussian depression plus a wider Gaussian rim, with the two widths chosen so the total volume is exactly zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "That detail matters. If you start with a simple bump, the grid keeps a permanent raised patch in the middle and the result looks like a spreading disc, not a ripple. A volume-conserving crater collapses, overshoots, and radiates a crest and a trough that travel outward together.\n",
    "\n",
    "Colour is vertical displacement, blue for a trough and red for a crest. The scale is fixed across all six panels, so the fade is real and not an auto-scaled illusion."
   ]
  },
