   "source": [
    "## 7. Energy spreads, so amplitude falls\n",
    "\n",
    "A circular wave carries a fixed amount of energy around a circumference that grows with radius. The energy per unit length therefore falls as $1/r$, and amplitude, which goes with its square root, falls as $1/\\sqrt{r}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "That is a quantitative prediction we can test. Track the ring peak as it travels outward and plot amplitude against radius on log axes. The fitted slope should sit near $-0.5$, and the product $A\\sqrt{r}$ should stay flat."
   ]
