    "## 8. What happens at the edge\n",
    "\n",
    "Two runs, identical except for the boundary. The first has a hard wall: displacement is pinned to zero at the rim, so the wave reflects and comes back. The second has an absorbing band, a layer where the loss coefficient ramps up, which swallows the outgoing ring.\n",
    "\n",
    "Neither is perfectly physical. A real tank has walls, so reflection is real if the tank is small. The point is that the boundary is a modelling choice, and the energy curve shows the difference in one number: the wall keeps almost everything, the absorbing rim gives most of it up.\n",
    "\n",
    "A five-point stencil is slightly anisotropic, which is why a long-running ring slowly squares off. That is a property of the grid, not of the water, and it is listed again in the limits at the end."
   ]
  },
